# AGENTS.md

## Local overrides

If `AGENTS.local.md` exists at the repo root, read it and let its instructions take precedence over
this file.

## Commands

`Taskfile.yml` wraps the common workflows -- run `task --list` to see them. These are the only
entrypoints; never bypass them by calling `cargo` directly for tasks that `task` covers -- the tasks
manage flags and tool chains.

- `task install` -- install the prek hooks.
- `task test` -- run the test suite via cargo-nextest plus doctests (append args after `--`).
- `task cov` -- run tests with coverage via cargo-llvm-cov.
- `task check` -- run every prek hook over all tracked and untracked files. Hooks that can fix
  (dprint, markdownlint) rewrite files in place. Run before committing. Tests run at pre-push, not
  here -- use `task test`.
- `task clean` -- remove build artifacts.

## Linter and type-checker configuration

Treat lint and type-check config as fixed. Never add to an ignore list, disable a rule, lower a
severity, or exclude a file to get a check passing -- fix the code instead. A suppression is
warranted only when the finding is a genuine false positive or the rule cannot apply (e.g. a
generated file, a documented upstream bug); then suppress at the narrowest scope -- an inline
`#[allow(...)]` with a reason -- not in the shared config. When the same inline directive keeps
recurring for the same rule, that is a signal the rule may deserve a config-level ignore -- propose
it to the user and wait for explicit approval; never promote a suppression into config on your own.

## Doc comments

Rust doc comments (`///` for items, `//!` for modules). None required on private functions. A
one-line doc comment is complete on its own when the signature says the rest. When a function needs
more than one line (a side effect, an invariant, a precondition, behavior the name does not convey),
use a multi-line doc comment with every section that applies: `# Arguments`, `# Returns`, `#
Errors`, `# Panics`. A public function that returns `Result` has `# Errors`, and one that can panic
has `# Panics`, even when the rest fits one line; clippy enforces both. A function you edit gets its
doc comment brought to this shape even if you did not write it. Tests: no doc comments on test
functions; the name carries the intent.

## Spelling (cspell)

Treat a cspell failure as a prompt to reword, not to grow the dictionary. Prefer plain words in
prose and identifiers. A word earns a `cspell.json` entry only when it comes from outside the
project and cannot be renamed -- command names, API identifiers, crate names, proper nouns, domain
vocabulary (e.g. `nextest`, `clippy`, `rustfmt`). In tests, never invent gibberish that needs a
suppression -- any real word works for filler data.
